{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## What was measured (2026-10-27 21:50–22:05 PDT)\n\nThis description was rewritten after investigation. The original diagnosis — \"the start path's de-dup key does not include the plan material fingerprint\" — is **refuted**; it is preserved below under \"The refuted diagnosis\" so the correction is auditable, because it was propagated into automation before it was checked.\n\n### The confirmed defect: `verify_completion_start`'s de-dup key omits the material fingerprint\n\n`_mcp_llm` keys the completion gate's singleflight on\n\n```python\nvariant = f\"graph={graph};source={source or 'attested'}\"\n```\n\nat both the async-start and synchronous call sites. There is **no material fingerprint** in it.\n\nThe completion-verifier attestation *does* bind material: `llm/plan_review/attest.py` validates a stored completion attestation by recomputing `current_material_fingerprint` and returns `{\"valid\": False, \"verdict\": \"stale-material\"}` when it has moved. So a `verify_completion_start` issued after a material edit (description or `file_impact`) de-duplicates onto a run that read the **earlier** material, and the caller receives a verdict computed against a plan that no longer exists.\n\nThe plan-review side already solved this. `_plan_review_variant` is\n\n```python\nf\"source={source or 'attested'};material={current_material_fingerprint(ticket_id)}\"\n```\n\nlanded by commit `864b6f56c91` (\"Key plan-review dedup on current plan material\", 2026-10-18, ticket `3b27-8f4b-90bd-44b2`), an ancestor of `origin/main`. Its documented `material=None` fallback — such calls de-duplicate only with each other, never with a run keyed on a real fingerprint — is the behaviour the completion side should mirror.\n\n## The refuted diagnosis, and the evidence against it\n\nThe original claim was that `review_plan_start` de-duplicated across a material change and replayed the prior run's verdict. Three independent facts contradict it:\n\n1. **No de-dup occurred.** The reproduction records job `…dbe7ada2` at step 2 and job `…41c67599` at step 4 — **different job ids**. A de-duplicated start attaches to the existing job and returns *its* id. Two ids mean the singleflight key did not match.\n2. **The key already carries the fingerprint**, and had for nine days before the observation (commit above). A change to that key would have fixed nothing.\n3. **`stale-material` is not a de-dup artifact.** Its only producers are `attest.py` lines 667 and 718, inside attestation *validation*, comparing the ticket's recorded attestation against current material. That is why the returned `signed_at` was step 2's timestamp: it is the step-2 attestation being reported stale, not a replayed run.\n\n## What remains unexplained — do not delete this\n\nThe original observation is real and is **not** accounted for by the confirmed defect above. On story `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`:\n\n- `set_file_impact` 13 → 14 paths, then `review_plan_start` returned a BLOCK whose `durable.verdict` was `stale-material` and whose `signed_at` was the *previous* run's.\n- `show_ticket` reported `plan_reviews: 0` — **no findings were produced**, so no fresh review ran.\n- `review_plan_start(force=True)` on the identical tree and SHA (`ee5253abfd4`) returned a certified PASS.\n- `gate_status` simultaneously reported `status: \"passed\"` and `findings.readable: true` for the BLOCK job, so the two surfaces an agent would use to sanity-check disagreed.\n\nTwo candidate mechanisms, neither distinguished:\n\n1. **A reuse short-circuit fired.** `llm/plan_review/__init__.py` calls `reuse.idempotent_reuse` then `reuse.verdict_reuse`; both are bypassed by `force`, which matches the force/no-force asymmetry exactly. But on inspection neither should fire: `idempotent_reuse` returns `None` when `claim_gate_check` is not ok (a stale attestation is not ok), and `verdict_reuse` requires the stored sidecar's `material_fingerprint` to still match. If one fired, the bug is in its guard.\n2. **A precheck ahead of the LLM** returned the validation result directly, which would explain the empty findings and the replayed `signed_at` without any reuse path firing.\n\nThese produce the same observable payload, so static reading cannot separate them; it needs one instrumented run. That run was deliberately not taken: a plan review is a billable 15–20 minute LLM operation and the hypothesis was not narrow enough to justify spending one.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Add the ticket's current material fingerprint to the completion-gate dedup variant at both `verify_completion_start` and the synchronous `verify_completion` call site, mirroring `_plan_review_variant` including its `material=None` fallback.\n2. Regression test: start a completion verification, mutate `file_impact`, start again without `force`, and assert the second call gets its own job rather than attaching to the first; plus a test pinning the variant string itself, including the `material=None` key.\n3. Leave the plan-review symptom open under \"What remains unexplained\" until someone can afford one instrumented reproduction. Do not change `_plan_review_variant` — it is already correct.\n\n**Out of scope:** the reuse short-circuits, `attest.py`'s validation, and the `gate_status` / `plan_review_status` disagreement. Each needs the instrumented run first.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `verify_completion_start` issued after the ticket's material changed does not de-dup onto a job started before that change.\n- [ ] Regression test as described, plus a test pinning the dedup variant including the `material=None` fallback.\n- [x] `review_plan_start` audited: its key has included the material fingerprint since `864b6f56c91`, and the reported symptom is not a de-dup failure.\n- [ ] The \"What remains unexplained\" section is carried forward — closing this ticket must not be read as explaining the plan-review BLOCK.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0, allowing for the two known arm64-macOS host failures tracked in `bb19-4776-46d2-4223` and `7f3d-ddc9-04d9-4861`.\n\nDiscovered while implementing story `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`.","title":"verify_completion_start de-dups across a material change; the plan-review symptom is unexplained"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793170362540124361,"uuid":"a27c38f7-2324-4dcb-a708-586bcf893e19"}